### Describe the bug

It is my understanding that a `Scroll` object uses the `MinSize` of its contained object to determine whether and how to display scrolling sliders, in either dimension (horizontal / vertical). It seems that when the underlying content is larger than the viewport, this value needs to be modified, by a factor depending on the ratio between the viewport dimension and the content dimension. In the provided MWE (or WE at least I hope), a 0.25 factor must be applied at some point (in a custom `MinSize()` method) for the sliders to appear properly when resizing the window smaller than the displayed white square. In another context (more widgets and containers in the window), I had to apply a 0.5 factor instead (no idea why).

### How to reproduce

1. Run the code below, without uncommenting the `MinSize()` method. A white square is displayed, centered, in the window. Initially no sliders are visible.
2. Resize the window below the dimensions of the square (at least on one dimension). One or two scrolling sliders must be visible.
3. Scroll to the right and/or the bottom : it scrolls farther than the limit of the square.
4. Uncomment the `MinSize()` method, run the code.
5. Perform the same steps, the sliders do not allow to scroll further than the limits of the object.

